ITEM 3 — Regime Detection and Regime-Conditional Coefficients
─────────────────────────────────────────
Location: export_json.py + History tab + Analytics tab

The scatter plot shows two visually distinct regimes: a low-vol 
tight cluster (2010–2018, daily_vol < ~4%) and a high-vol extended 
diagonal (2019–present). A single OLS regression is systematically 
wrong in both. The percentile rank also spans both eras, making P83 
today not comparable to P83 in 2014.

In export_json.py:
Use the coder subagent to:
  - Define regimes using a simple threshold: 
    Regime A (structural) = daily_vol_pct < 4.5 OR year < 2019
    Regime B (elevated) = daily_vol_pct >= 4.5 AND year >= 2019
    (This threshold is empirical — validate it produces a clean split 
    using the scatter data before finalizing)
  - For each regime, fit separate OLS: margin ~ daily_vol_pct
    Emit: { regime_a: {slope, intercept, r2, n}, 
            regime_b: {slope, intercept, r2, n} }
  - Tag every historical row with its regime label
  - Compute percentile ranks BOTH all-history and within-current-regime
    Emit current_pct_alltime and current_pct_regime in the summary JSON
  - Write regime model params to docs/data/regime_model.json
  - Add regime label to each row in the history JSON

In index.html:
Use the coder subagent to:
  - On the History tab scatter plot, color points by regime 
    (blue = Regime A, orange = Regime B)
  - Draw two regression lines (one per regime) with ±1σ residual bands
  - On the Analytics tab header stats row, replace the single percentile 
    rank with two stacked values: "P82 all-time | P71 current regime"
  - Add a regime indicator chip near the NORMAL/MONITOR badge showing 
    which regime is currently active, with a tooltip explaining the 
    two regimes and their coefficient differences

